<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">
  <PropertyGroup>
    <OutputType>Exe</OutputType>
    <TargetFramework>net10.0-windows</TargetFramework>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
    <ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>
  </PropertyGroup>
  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\St4i.EdgeCore\St4i.EdgeCore.csproj" />
    <!-- 🔴 Task D-7c — the owner's ruling of 2026-08-03: direct RS-485 goes into ALL THREE hosts, so all three
         reference St4i.EdgeCore.Serial and all three ship System.IO.Ports.dll. The accepted cost (a
         gateway-only site carrying an OS-specific dependency it never uses) is the owner's call and is
         recorded in the Đợt D ledger.

         🔴 WHAT THIS REFERENCE DOES AND DOES NOT DO IN *THIS* HOST, measured rather than assumed, because the
         ruling's stated basis was that EdgeWorker.cs uses FleetHost/ConnectorRegistry and it does not:
         St4i.EdgeService has NO connector plumbing whatsoever — no ConnectorRegistry, no IConnectorFactory,
         no connectors.json reader. EdgeWorker.ExecuteAsync builds one SimulatedDriver from fleet.json and
         nothing else; ConnectorRegistry and FleetHost are St4i.EngineApi types and are referenced nowhere
         under src/ outside that project. So this reference makes the serial transport AVAILABLE to this host
         and nothing more: no code path here can open a COM port today, and giving it one means giving this
         host a connector registry first, which is a separate piece of work nobody has scoped.

         🔴 TASK E-3 DID THAT PIECE OF WORK, PARTLY, AND THE PARAGRAPH ABOVE IS NOW HALF FALSE. Corrected
         rather than deleted, because it is the record of what the ruling was based on:
           - "no ConnectorRegistry, no IConnectorFactory, no connectors.json reader" — NO LONGER TRUE.
             EdgeConnectors.cs reads connectors.json (through the parser E-3 moved down to
             St4i.EdgeCore.Config so both hosts share ONE reader) and populates a ConnectorRegistry, which
             E-2 moved down to St4i.EdgeCore. EdgeWorker runs N drivers off it via EdgeAgentPipelines.
           - "ConnectorRegistry and FleetHost are St4i.EngineApi types" — ConnectorRegistry moved in E-2.
             FleetHost is still EngineApi's, and FleetCore — the lifecycle it shells — is now `internal` on
             St4i.EdgeCore precisely so THIS host cannot reach its unguarded write path (blueprint §3).
           - "no code path here can open a COM port today" — STILL TRUE, and still for a scoping reason
             rather than a capability one: an RTU entry in connectors.json is refused BY NAME here, because
             the multidrop fan-out (ModbusMultidropRegistration → RtuBusConfiguration) has not moved and is
             not a leaf. See EdgeConnectors' own remarks and blueprint §11. So this reference still only
             makes the serial transport AVAILABLE.

         Stated here, and asserted in the honest form by
         SerialDependencyScopingTests.EdgeServiceDeployment_CarriesSystemIoPorts_…, so that the shipped
         artifact and the record of why it ships agree. -->
    <ProjectReference Include="..\St4i.EdgeCore.Serial\St4i.EdgeCore.Serial.csproj" />
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Extensions.Hosting" Version="9.0.0" />
  </ItemGroup>
</Project>
